 "interpretation": [
  {
   "heading": "0. The manager pays off once it learns to choose -- most with a shared critic",
   "body": "v1 paid the manager the game reward only; most goals cannot be reached in 8 steps, so the actor ignored them, the manager stayed uniform, and it scored below B2 (7.13). v1.1 pays both levels when a goal is reached; its manager learned to choose and it scored 8.53. v1.2 replaces the manager's own critic with one two-headed critic shared with the bottom actor, normalises the game and goal rewards separately, and holds each goal until reached: 9.73, and the first regular stone pickaxe (23%). Its manager favours goals it cannot complete yet (iron pickaxe, iron sword, diamond), which pay the bottom actor for gathering every ingredient on the way -- plausibly why it stocks up instead of spending its wood at once. All three trailed B2 early and overtook it between 350k and 550k steps. One seed per run."
  },
  {
   "heading": "1. Masking impossible actions is the largest gain so far",
   "body": "B2 7.60 vs honest map 5.87, same model and reward. Experiment A on its final checkpoint shows why: buttons that sat at exactly 0% in the states where they would work now get pressed there (furnace 3.2% over 599 states, placing stone 2.4%, placing a table 75%, the wood pickaxe 100%), and every no-effect press is now a basic action (DO, NOOP, a blocked move). Once a useless press can no longer teach the agent that a button is bad, it presses that button when it works."
  },
  {
   "heading": "2. Telling helps, masking helps more",
   "body": "B1 (flags as input) reaches 6.90: both wood crafts survive in one run for the first time and the furnace comes off zero, but placing stone stays at 0.0% across 452 chances and about 6% of presses still go to impossible buttons. The information helps; removing the suppressing gradient helps more. Masking changes the action space relative to published Craftax baselines, so it is reported as a separate arm."
  },
  {
   "heading": "3. The next wall is sequencing, not suppression",
   "body": "In B2 the stone pickaxe was craftable in only 5 of 5,167 evaluation steps: the agent mines stone but rarely returns to a table holding both wood and stone. That gates iron and every 3-5-point achievement after it. It is a planning problem, so the next experiment should target multi-step sequences (experiment D, return-then-explore, or a sub-goal signal), not the action distribution."
  },
  {
   "heading": "4. Survival is still unsolved",
   "body": "Lifespans stay at or below the random band, except B1 (311, mean pulled up by a few long episodes; median 265). The better runs die earlier: B2 246, v1.2 221 steps, with half of v1.2's deaths to mobs or lava -- the more it does, the more it is exposed -- and thirst first to run out in most of the rest. Manager v1.3 targets this with survival goals and a survival potential."
  },
  {
   "heading": "5. What to run next",
   "body": "Training now: manager v1.3 (v1.2 plus survival goals and a survival potential, aimed at the thirst deaths). Queued: B3 (B2 with the action mask learned instead of transcribed from the rules, so no game rules at test time). Then the best manager version with B3's learned mask, and second seeds of the leaders, since every comparison here is single-seed."
  }
 ],
